=== CO5 TASK A2: FOLLOW THE APPLICATION PROTOCOLS ===
A2.1 — SMTP Command Sequence & Reply Codes:
  S: 220 mail.learn.edu ESMTP Ready          (Server greeting)
  C: EHLO student-laptop.local               (Client identifies itself)
  S: 250-mail.learn.edu Hello                (Server accepts EHLO)
  C: MAIL FROM:<santhosh@learn.edu>          (Envelope sender)
  S: 250 2.1.0 Ok                            (Sender accepted)
  C: RCPT TO:<faculty@learn.edu>             (Envelope recipient)
  S: 250 2.1.5 Ok                            (Recipient accepted)
  C: DATA                                    (Start message body)
  S: 354 End data with <CR><LF>.<CR><LF>     (Ready for payload)
  C: Subject: Lab Report\r\n...\r\n.         (Body terminated with '.')
  S: 250 2.0.0 Ok: queued as A1B2C           (Message queued)
  C: QUIT                                    (Close session)
  S: 221 2.0.0 Bye                           (Connection closed)
  POP3 (delete after download) vs IMAP: POP3 deletes mail from server after phone downloads it ->
    laptop and lab PC never see it. IMAP keeps folders/flags synchronised on the server across all devices.

A2.2 — Active vs Passive FTP, SFTP/HTTPS & SNMP:
  - Active FTP fails : Client opens control TCP/21; server initiates data TCP/20 BACK to client's ephemeral port -> blocked by NAT/firewall.
  - Passive FTP works: Client sends PASV and initiates BOTH control and data connections outbound.
  - SFTP / HTTPS     : Encrypts credentials & payload (FTP transmits passwords & data in plaintext).
  - SNMP GET         : Manager polls OID 1.3.6.1.2.1.2.2.1.10.1 (ifInOctets.1) via UDP/161; agent replies GetResponse.
  - SNMP TRAP        : Router proactively sends unsolicited UDP/162 linkDown (OID 1.3.6.1.6.3.1.1.5.3) on link failure.

A2.3 — Browser DevTools Slow-Page Timings:
  - 'DNS Lookup' (>100 ms) indicates uncached/slow resolver; 'Waiting (TTFB)' indicates backend/DB/TLS stall;
    'Content Download' indicates large unbundled payloads or low throughput.

--- Alternative Concept Analysis: Webmail HTTPS vs IMAP (50 Messages) ---
  - IMAP (headers first): 50 headers x ~1 KB = ~50 KB; fetches bodies only when clicked.
  - Webmail HTTPS       : HTML/JS/CSS framework (~1-2 MB) + 50 JSON summaries (~100 KB), but easily cached.
  - Easier to secure    : Webmail HTTPS (single port 443, modern OAuth2/MFA/CSP/WAF without exposing IMAP 993).
